{
  "evidence": "TEXT_G: - Leave the EU and repeal the European Communities Act to restore UK sovereignty and national control; - Aim for near‑zero net immigration with stricter rules, Britishness/language tests, and faster removals; - Tougher sentencing, more prisons, and defence of homeowners; - Reclaim UK waters and prioritise national defence/sovereignty over EU structures; - Defend traditional institutions (Commons, Crown, independent Lords), English votes for English laws; - Repeal the Human Rights Act and reject the EU Charter; - Emphasis on British history, values, and national pride. TEXT_D: - Strong internationalism and EU integration (support EU Constitution, prepare to join the euro); - Civil liberties and equality focus (repeal control orders, defend jury trials, Single Equality Act, hate‑crime units); - More permissive asylum approach (fair decisions, allow asylum seekers to work); - Green agenda (carbon tax, 20% renewables, oppose replacing nuclear, congestion/road‑user charging); - Political reform and devolution (STV, votes at 16).",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile is moderately TAN‑leaning, prioritising national identity, order, and tradition over green/internationalist liberalism. TEXT_G argues from sovereignty, restrictive immigration, law‑and‑order, and defence of traditional institutions (TAN), whereas TEXT_D argues from civil liberties, environmentalism, and internationalism (GAL), so TEXT_G aligns more closely with the profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
